  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">

      <p>Iran's Persian Gulf Strait Authority published a formal map on May 22 delineating its claimed management zone across the Strait of Hormuz — from Kuh-e Mobarak to the south of Fujairah at the eastern entrance and from the end of Qeshm Island to Umm al-Quwain at the western entrance, covering waters the UAE and Oman regard as their sovereign territory (Euronews, May 22). The same day, Bahrain, Kuwait, Qatar, Saudi Arabia, and the UAE submitted a joint letter to the IMO urging commercial vessels not to engage with the PGSA or use Iran's designated route. Independently verified commercial transits stood at 2 vessels on May 22 (Hormuz Strait Monitor), against a pre-war baseline of 95/day; Lloyd's war-risk premiums remained above 5% of hull value. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span></p>

      <p>The fifth round of US-Iran talks concluded in Rome on May 23 without a signed agreement. Araghchi publicly characterized remaining differences with Washington as "deep and significant" (Kaieteur News, citing Iranian FM, May 23). The Omani mediator described the outcome as "some but not conclusive progress" and stated the parties would attempt to "clarify the remaining issues in the coming days." Araghchi separately told UN Secretary-General Guterres that US "excessive demands" constitute the principal obstacle. Iranian Foreign Ministry spokesman Baghaei confirmed via IRNA that nuclear enrichment details are not yet on the table. No Witkoff/Araghchi direct meeting was confirmed for the Rome round or its aftermath. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span></p>

      <p>Israel's Knesset passed the preliminary reading of the government-backed dissolution bill 110-0 on May 20 — a unanimous margin that included both coalition and opposition lawmakers — with Prime Minister Netanyahu absent from the chamber (Times of Israel, Jerusalem Post, Haaretz, May 20). The bill does not specify an election date; the Knesset House Committee will set it, with the election required to fall between three and five months after final passage. Netanyahu, who reportedly prefers October, was described by multiple Israeli outlets as engaged in side-negotiations with Haredi parties to slow or redirect the bill. The dissolution process still requires committee review plus first, second, and third readings. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span>, supports <span class="cite">F2</span> without move</p>

      <p>The Lebanon 45-day ceasefire extension, announced May 15, remained technically in force through EOD May 22, meeting the condition for RESOLVE-NO on <span class="cite">F4</span>. IDF Chief of Staff Zamir stated during a visit to southern Lebanon troops that there "is no ceasefire" — an operational assessment reflecting ongoing low-intensity exchanges, not a formal government withdrawal of US or Lebanese sponsorship. IDF launched strikes on 30+ Hezbollah infrastructure targets in southern Lebanon; Hezbollah fired rockets at IDF troops in Lebanon. Neither side's activity crossed the resolution threshold of sustained exchanges exceeding 50 launches per day from each side simultaneously with formal acknowledgment of breakdown by both the US and Lebanon. The Pentagon security track remained scheduled for May 29 and political talks for June 2-3. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span></p>

      <p>Secretary of State Rubio, speaking May 21-22, described "progress" in Iran talks while emphasizing "more work to be done" (Detroit News, May 22). US Polymarket odds for Hormuz traffic returning to normal by end of June 2026 stood below 25%. Iran's PGSA toll mechanism — with reported fees of up to $2 million per transit payable in Chinese yuan — has attracted shadow-fleet operators but not publicly named non-sanctioned companies; Windward.ai and Lloyd's List have not confirmed independent third-party PGSA-cleared transits distinct from IRGC-linked tonnage. → supports <span class="cite">F6</span> without move</p>

    </div>
  </div>

