  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>

      <p>IRNA and Tasnim have framed the Rome fifth round as a demonstration of Iranian procedural discipline rather than a failed negotiation. Foreign Ministry spokesman Baghaei's IRNA-cited statement that nuclear enrichment "is not being discussed at this stage" serves a dual function in state-media coverage: it rebuffs any domestic reading that Iran is capitulating on the nuclear file, while leaving open the possibility of future discussion. Tasnim, citing a source close to negotiations, ran the line "progress has been made on some issues, but no agreement until all matters resolved" — a formulation calibrated to keep domestic audiences invested in the process without raising expectations of imminent concession. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span> without move</p>

      <p>The PGSA's May 22 jurisdictional map received prominent placement on IRNA and Press TV, framed as a sovereign assertion rather than a tactical escalation. IRNA ran the zone coordinates without independent legal commentary; Fars presented Gulf-state IMO objections as "foreign interference." State media did not carry Araghchi's "deep and significant gaps" statement in its strongest form — the phrase was sourced to Iranian news agency wires picked up by international outlets; domestic coverage emphasized Araghchi's note that Iran is "on a reasonable path." → supports <span class="cite">F1</span>, <span class="cite">F3</span> without move</p>

      <p>Kayhan, the hardline daily, published an editorial characterizing the PGSA as a permanent structural change rather than a wartime measure, arguing that any post-war settlement must incorporate Iranian toll authority as a fait accompli. This framing creates domestic political costs for any Iranian negotiator who later concedes PGSA sovereignty in a deal. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

    </div>
  </div>

